<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lobbes Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lobbes Abbey was a Benedictine monastery on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a> in Hainaut, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, founded in the seventh century by Saint Landelin. In the tenth and eleventh centuries it was one of the leading centres of learning in Lotharingia, under abbots such as Folcuin and Heriger. It was suppressed after the French invasion of the 1790s; its Romanesque church of Saint Ursmer survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lobbes-abbey.jpg" alt="Lobbes Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobbes_-_La_Portelette_-_JPG1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The early history of Lobbes is known in some detail because its annals survive. According to them, Landelin, a young man of a well-to-do family from Bapaume, had led a band of brigands before he repented and founded a monastery on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a>, at the place where he had sinned. The community grew quickly, and Landelin was abbot until 680. His successor Ursmar preached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> among the largely pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, and under Ursmar and the abbots after him, Ermin, Abel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> and Theodulph, Lobbes became the leading monastery of the region. Under the sixth abbot, Anson, from 776 to 800, its school became widely known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 864 Hucbert, brother-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II of Lotharingia</a>, became lay abbot and nearly ruined the house. His successor Franco was both abbot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Liège, an arrangement that lasted until 960, and the abbey became largely a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the bishops of Liège, though it lay in the diocese of Cambrai. Hungarian raiders burned it in 954. Its reputation recovered under Folcuin, Heriger and Hugo, abbot from 1033 to 1053. After the wars of Godfrey III against Emperor Henry III, the monks toured Hainaut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> with the relics of Saint Ursmar in 1060 to regain their estates, settling feuds as they went, as the Miracula sancti Ursmari tells; this took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-and-truce-of-god">Peace of God</a> movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The abbey declined until the reform movement of the Bursfelde Congregation revived it in the 15th century, and in 1569 it joined the Benedictine Congregation of Exempt Monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. In 1794 French revolutionary troops expelled the last abbot, Vulgise de Vignron, and 43 monks, and on 11 May troops under General Louis Charbonnier burned the abbey and its library, destroying thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and books. The abbey was dissolved by the law of 2 September 1796. The Lobbes Bible, completed in 1084, survives in the Tournai Seminary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lobbes Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most of its people were peasants growing wheat and other grains under the lordship of nobles and of rich abbeys such as <b>Lobbes</b>, Saint-Ghislain, Maroilles and Crespin, and the chapter of noble canonesses of Sainte-Waudru in Mons was one of the county's leading landowners.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
